<header><p class="st-kicker">other · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Shanghai International Settlement</h1><p class="st-meta">1863 CE – 1943 CE · capital Shanghai</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The self-governing foreign enclave at the heart of Shanghai, run by a council of foreign ratepayers, which made the city China's commercial capital.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> was opened to foreign trade by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-nanjing">Treaty of Nanking</a>, a British settlement was laid out along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-bund">Bund</a> in 1845 and an American one at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hongkou">Hongkou</a> in 1848. In 1863 the two merged as the International Settlement, while the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/shanghai-french-concession">Shanghai French Concession</a> stayed separate.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Settlement was governed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai-municipal-council">Shanghai Municipal Council</a>, elected by foreign property owners, with its own police and volunteer corps, while foreigners enjoyed extraterritoriality. Most residents were Chinese, but they had no seats on the council until 1928. Banks, trading houses and factories made it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China's</a> leading business centre, and the shooting of protesters by its police sparked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/may-thirtieth-movement">May Thirtieth Movement</a> in 1925.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> occupied the Settlement in December 1941. In 1943 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a> renounced their rights to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nationalist-government">Nationalist government</a>, and Japan transferred control to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/wang-jingwei-regime">Wang Jingwei regime</a>; after 1945 it was fully Chinese.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/shanghai-international-settlement.jpg" alt="Shanghai International Settlement" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Ericmetro and others · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Shanghai_International_Settlement_pre-WWI.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1863 CE – 1943 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Shanghai</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Chinese folk religion, Buddhism, Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Shanghainese, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>International Settlement of Shanghai</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1903&amp;focus=shanghai-international-settlement" aria-label="Shanghai International Settlement on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7818 1040 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Shanghai International Settlement lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8138 1296L8221 1549"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8221 1549h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8138 1296h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8138 1296h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Shanghai. <a href="/atlas?year=1903&amp;focus=shanghai-international-settlement">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
